00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:55.030
Cloud that's going to
enable what we're saying,

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:59.437
enable unleashing
web-based innovation?

00:11:59.437 --> 00:12:01.770
DANIEL O'PREY: So what does
that mean for your business?

00:12:01.770 --> 00:12:04.730
So DAML, the Digital
Asset Modeling Language,

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:06.470
is a new,
small-contract language

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:09.140
developed by Digital
Asset to, as Shaul said,

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:13.200
abstract away the
underlying infrastructure.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:16.190
So you don't need to worry
about the cryptography,

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:19.850
or the distribution, or
notifications, or privacy.

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:22.790
You just focus on the
business of your business.

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:25.060
What drives value
to your customers?

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:27.860
And let the language
and the platform

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:32.090
take care of everything
behind the scenes.

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:34.160
In addition to that,
we announced yesterday

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:37.160
that DAML platform as a
service on Google Cloud--

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:40.250
similarly, just lets you
focus on the application,

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:41.830
build something
that delivers value,

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.130
do not worry about
configuring the platform--

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:47.900
configuring the network,
deploying the software,

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:51.830
and the underlying
infrastructure.

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:55.370
So together, this
enables rapid prototyping

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:57.740
for your business,
shorter time to market,

00:12:57.740 --> 00:12:59.760
building distributed
ledger applications that

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.480
can deliver value faster and
sooner than otherwise possible,

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:08.330
and enabling you to be
more agile to changing

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:12.530
customer demands, regulatory
requirements, or market forces.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:17.780
SHAUL KFIR: So you've all
heard about public blockchains.

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:20.820
And in public blockchains,
what those have achieved--

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:22.490
essentially getting
data integrity

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:27.290
across a large-scale
deployment, or getting consensus

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:31.490
over data across a
large group of entities,

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:35.780
be it people or companies,
and synchronizing those views.

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:38.970
And that is a huge achievement.

00:13:38.970 --> 00:13:41.390
What most public blockchains
don't deal with very well

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:43.067
is data confidentiality.

00:13:43.067 --> 00:13:44.150
We get the data integrity.

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:47.150
We get that synchronized
view, but everyone

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:48.200
gets that same view.

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.260
And there are varying
degrees of confidentiality.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:52.430
Some do get it right.

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.210
But what they struggle
dealing with is very complex,

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:58.310
or generic workflows--
the types of workflows

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:01.180
we have in most large
enterprises today.

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:02.180
Now, don't get me wrong.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:04.850
This is a huge thing,
and it's a great solution

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:06.880
to a specific set of problems.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.882
But it's for that
specific set of problems.

00:14:08.882 --> 00:14:10.340
What we did at
Digital Asset-- when

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:13.350
we started, we were a group
of cryptocurrency enthusiastic

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.000
developers, and
we said, how do we

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.170
take the same inspiration, or
the same types of solutions,

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:20.320
and apply it to a
different domain,

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:25.370
to the domain of large
cross-enterprise workflows?

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:27.770
Now, in the private
distributed-ledger space,

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:30.920
we see a lot of attempts
to achieve confidentiality

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:34.880
by really siloing data or using
techniques such as encryption,

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:37.490
zero-knowledge proofs,
or overlay networks

00:14:37.490 --> 00:14:42.220
on top of a base network to
compartmentalize the data.

00:14:42.220 --> 00:14:46.100
What most of those solutions
do is they trade off integrity,

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:47.900
or in many cases liveness.

00:14:47.900 --> 00:14:49.940
And it's a bit too
much to dive deep.

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:52.430
I would love to go in
deeper in this talk,

00:14:52.430 --> 00:14:55.160
but we talked much more
in-depth about this in our blog.

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:57.320
And I urge everyone
to go to DAML.com

00:14:57.320 --> 00:14:59.510
to see how we thought
about these trade-offs

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:04.790
between integrity,
privacy, and liveness.

00:15:04.790 --> 00:15:08.030
So what did we do with the
Digital Asset platform?

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:10.970
Through a series
since 2014 of working

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:15.470
with very large, potential
customers, and today customers,

00:15:15.470 --> 00:15:18.860
we went through a series of
increasingly complex MVPs

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:20.570
on building a platform.

00:15:20.570 --> 00:15:24.230
And we actually started from
those public blockchain roots,

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:26.570
which was my background
and my alma mater

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:29.120
in zero-knowledge proofs.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.704
What we actually found out is
that the hardest problem that

00:15:31.704 --> 00:15:34.120
needed to get solved was the
problem of data architecture,

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:37.730
of how do you structure your
data in a way that preserves

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:40.520
integrity across the distributed
system where not everyone has

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:43.040
all data-- so it also
preserve privacy--

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:45.380
and also preserves
liveness-- that the network

00:15:45.380 --> 00:15:49.490
can't stop when you maintain
those other two properties,

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:52.580
the integrity and the privacy?

00:15:52.580 --> 00:15:54.740
What we did is we
built a formal modeling

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:58.100
of what the data should look
like on a distributed ledger.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:16:00.200
Then we built a domain-specific
language, again,

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:03.125
through a series of
MVPs and understanding,

00:16:03.125 --> 00:16:05.510
deeper, what those use
cases are of our clients.

00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:07.400
We built a
domain-specific language

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:09.320
that forces the
developer to structure

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:11.780
the data in such a way that
is amenable to distributed

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:12.702
ledgers.

00:16:12.702 --> 00:16:14.910
On the other side, we wanted
it to be very efficient,

00:16:14.910 --> 00:16:17.660
so it lets the developer only
think about their business use

00:16:17.660 --> 00:16:19.190
case, about their
business workflow,

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:21.750
and not have to think
about the infrastructure

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:24.240
that they are deploying to.

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.120
What we got to is
a platform that

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:27.860
does real-time
reconciliation across all

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:31.280
of the nodes in the
network, is auditable,

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:33.710
analyzable, extensible.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:35.360
And when you build
workflows, you

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:38.250
can use those common
workflows and reuse them.

00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:40.130
So if someone builds
a library or a module,

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:41.910
you can then reuse
that workflow,

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:46.850
and again, build on top of
the shoulders of giants.

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:49.460
DAML, or the Digital Asset
modeling language, that's

00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.080
the language that we created.

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.650
And that's the surface, or
the API, the developer sees.

00:16:53.650 --> 00:16:55.910
They build using this
language, and that

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:59.660
gets deployed to pretty
complex infrastructure.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:03.020
DAML is a language
that was built

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.880
bottom-up, purpose built for the
domain of distributed ledgers.

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:08.880
What we've seen in the
public blockchain space--

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:10.755
and I'm sure everyone
has heard about these--

00:17:10.755 --> 00:17:14.060
are very public bugs that cost
stakeholders in the hundreds

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.380
of millions of dollars.

00:17:15.380 --> 00:17:19.040
And that's simply something
we can't allow in our domain.

00:17:19.040 --> 00:17:20.720
Some clients are
using RSDK today

00:17:20.720 --> 00:17:23.660
to model businesses
that run daily volumes

00:17:23.660 --> 00:17:25.220
in the trillions of dollars.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:27.410
So a typical transaction
size is in the hundreds

00:17:27.410 --> 00:17:28.700
of millions of dollars.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:29.740
We can't allow a bug.

00:17:29.740 --> 00:17:32.935
Now, those bugs that happen
in the public blockchain

00:17:32.935 --> 00:17:35.060
space aren't because the
developers aren't skilled.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:36.970
These were very, very
skilled developers.

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:38.930
It's because they
lacked the right tools

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.870
to build these types of
workflows in a secure manner.

00:17:44.312 --> 00:17:45.770
There are specific
domains that are

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:49.100
mission critical, that
require the type of frameworks

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.450
for that.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:54.230
In a prior life, I was
operating and maintaining

00:17:54.230 --> 00:17:56.810
missile defense systems that
were protecting my life,

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:58.790
protecting my shipmates' lives.

00:17:58.790 --> 00:18:01.760
And I'm extremely grateful
that none of those

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.777
were written in JavaScript.

00:18:04.777 --> 00:18:05.860
And we took that approach.

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.651
We took that approach
of building something

00:18:07.651 --> 00:18:11.600
that's purpose built
for this domain.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:15.560
What we came up with
was DAML, and that's

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:19.254
a language that really abstracts
away the ledger details.

00:18:19.254 --> 00:18:20.670
And that's the
offering that we're

00:18:20.670 --> 00:18:24.990
making available on Google
Cloud as of yesterday.

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.970
DANIEL O'PREY: So what
can you model in DAML?

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:29.100
Our customers and
our partners have

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:31.440
built distributed
ledger applications

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:34.350
across many
different industries.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:36.540
For example-- well
actually, when

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:39.030
you think about small
contracts, what they really

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:42.090
are at their core, when you take
away the hype and the fluff,

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:46.330
are shared business processes
that span across organizations.

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:50.280
As such, the potential for use
of DAML and small contracts

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:53.730
more broadly, within
commerce, is absolutely huge.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:58.470
It can cover every asset,
transaction, and workflow.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.210
So for example,
we've been working

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:03.570
with a large clearinghouse
in the health care space

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:06.120
to do straight-through
processing for claims

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.600
eligibility and
payments, to make sure

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:13.110
that the payer and provider and
everyone throughout that value

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:17.490
train sees the same
data in real time.

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:19.930
Medical records, as
Blythe mentioned earlier,

00:19:19.930 --> 00:19:24.090
can be synchronized amongst
insurers, patients, doctors,

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:28.200
and hospitals, and selectively
reveal only the pertinent piece

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:31.660
of information to each
of those entities.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:34.580
Our partners have been
exploring in supply chain,

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:39.470
pharmaceutical supply chain,
through the manufacture,

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:43.445
transport, and sale of
drugs, many of which

00:19:43.445 --> 00:19:46.070
there is a large problem
with counterfeiting,

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:49.670
to ensure that what you receive
at the end of that supply chain

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:53.980
is what was produced
at the beginning.

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.080
Complex processes
can be coordinated

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:59.560
between e-commerce sites and
fulfillment centers and payment

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:02.860
providers and down to
the shipping company

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:05.830
to coordinate all
across the value chain,

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:09.495
across multiple different,
distinct legal entities.

00:20:09.495 --> 00:20:10.660
And many more.

00:20:10.660 --> 00:20:13.390
So we've been working with
partners on managing software

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:18.700
licenses, simplifying the
mortgage application process.

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:25.000
For insurance, reinsurance,
as well as even the issuance,

00:20:25.000 --> 00:20:27.130
secondary market,
and redemption-- the

00:20:27.130 --> 00:20:29.125
use of concert tickets.

00:20:29.125 --> 00:20:30.500
So as you can see,
there's really

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:34.240
a broad application for DAML.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:38.770
DAML can be used for any
multi-party workflow.

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:40.370
The largest traction
we've seen so far

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:42.800
has been within
financial services.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:46.540
So you're going to see here a
list of some of our investors,

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:49.210
each of which are exploring
using DAML, building

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:52.270
DAML-driven applications
in their own way.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:57.040
Banks, stock exchange groups,
large technology vendors,

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:59.770
global-systems
integrators are building

00:20:59.770 --> 00:21:02.860
DAML-driven applications
for internal use cases

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:05.200
across business
lines, bilaterally

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:09.130
between two entities,
or even as replacements

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:11.620
for entire markets.

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:13.930
And these use cases, even
within financial services,

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:15.340
are very, very varied--

00:21:15.340 --> 00:21:18.730
cash equities, repo,
collateral management.

00:21:18.730 --> 00:21:20.770
There is a lot of
very, very complex

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:24.070
behind-the-scenes
processes that most of us

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:26.260
are blissfully
unaware of but are

00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:30.380
vital to the functioning
of the global economy.

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:33.550
So these are some of the most
highly-regulated and most

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:35.410
demanding customers
in the world.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:37.330
The goal is if we
can solve for them,

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:41.900
we can solve for any
commercial use case.

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:44.380
And I know that this
is no longer a case

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:46.810
of will DLT see adoption?

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:47.900
But when.

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:50.710
And that leads onto
a great case study,

00:21:50.710 --> 00:21:52.810
one of our marquee clients--

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:55.150
the Australian
Securities Exchange,

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:59.110
the ASX, who we've been working
with for just over three years

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:00.770
now.

00:22:00.770 --> 00:22:02.872
So the ASX are
doing this for real.

00:22:02.872 --> 00:22:04.330
They're not playing
around anymore.

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:09.630
They have committed to take into
production a full replacement,

00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:13.660
on national scale, our first
financial-market-infrastructure

00:22:13.660 --> 00:22:16.530
project using a
distributed ledger.

00:22:16.530 --> 00:22:17.880
This is not a POC.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:18.820
This is not a pilot.

00:22:18.820 --> 00:22:23.640
This is a real production build
that is going to production.

00:22:23.640 --> 00:22:25.720
And it's not a
Greenfield application.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:27.450
It's not a peripheral service.

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:28.950
This is an actual replacement.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:31.050
At the end of this
process, they will

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.840
be switching off their
clearing-and-settlement system

00:22:33.840 --> 00:22:37.170
for cash equities and
replacing it entirely

00:22:37.170 --> 00:22:41.300
with the Digital Asset
platform and DAML.

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:42.980
So this isn't mangos
on a blockchain.

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:45.680
This isn't collectible kitties.

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:48.560
This is an application
that really matters.

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:52.220
If this goes down, the
Australian economy is affected.

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:54.510
People's pensions are affected.

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:57.500
And it's a real
mission-critical system.

00:22:57.500 --> 00:23:01.880
Every single stock in Australia
will be cleared and settled

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:04.152
on a blockchain.

00:23:04.152 --> 00:23:07.010
And now, we're excited
to bring that technology

00:23:07.010 --> 00:23:08.900
to Google Cloud.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:12.200
So yesterday, we were delighted
to announce that Digital Asset

00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:14.930
and Google Cloud have
been collaborating

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:16.790
to bring the technology
that you've been

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:20.240
hearing about today to GCP.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:23.870
We announced that Google Cloud
have joined the private beta

00:23:23.870 --> 00:23:25.100
of our developer program.

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:27.680
And as such, their
solution architects

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:31.070
can explore with their
clients applications

00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:33.230
within various
different industries,

00:23:33.230 --> 00:23:36.420
using DAML and our technology.

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:39.620
We also announced the
addition of a DAML pass,

00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:43.160
the platform as a service,
hosted on Google Cloud.

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:47.000
So similarly, customers can
just spin that up and quickly

00:23:47.000 --> 00:23:49.600
and easily start
building applications.

00:23:49.600 --> 00:23:51.530
And later this
year, Google will be

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:54.740
hosting a series of
developer conferences focused

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:56.510
around DAML.

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:59.880
And if you are interested in
joining, please visit DAML--

00:23:59.880 --> 00:24:02.400
D-A-M-L-- dot-com and
registering your interest

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:03.704
there.

00:24:03.704 --> 00:24:05.870
SHAUL KFIR: So as Dan said,
we're extremely excited.

00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:08.950
For me, this is a bit of
a personal excitement.

00:24:08.950 --> 00:24:10.450
It's paying it forward.

00:24:10.450 --> 00:24:13.160
Just like I said,
I was mind blown

00:24:13.160 --> 00:24:15.620
when I started web development,
seeing how fast you

00:24:15.620 --> 00:24:16.537
can deploy a business.

00:24:16.537 --> 00:24:18.870
I'm hoping that people will
be using this in the future.

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:21.260
Well, I know people will be
using this in the future,

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.440
in order to deploy their
businesses at much larger scale

00:24:24.440 --> 00:24:29.400
and really bring that pace, that
they can do things much faster.

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:32.620
So I wish we had
time for a live demo.

00:24:32.620 --> 00:24:34.380
I'm a coder, and
that's what we do.

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:36.960
But the next best
thing, at least,

00:24:36.960 --> 00:24:39.860
we'll talk a little bit
about what this offering is.

00:24:39.860 --> 00:24:43.880
So first of all, at the
top layer, what you can do

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:46.970
is build applications using all
of the best of the Google Cloud

00:24:46.970 --> 00:24:50.060
infrastructure, things
like Google Cloud Functions

00:24:50.060 --> 00:24:53.080
and TensorFlow and Spanner.

00:24:53.080 --> 00:24:58.040
Now, I can talk for hours
about why these offerings are

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:00.350
so fit for purpose for
building applications

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:02.450
that connect into a
distributed ledger.

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:04.670
But just to give
a sense, many of

00:25:04.670 --> 00:25:07.010
the workflows in the financial
world that we're working

00:25:07.010 --> 00:25:11.330
with need data analysis,
need risk analysis,

00:25:11.330 --> 00:25:14.180
and many types of optimization.

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:15.860
And those are things
that we believe

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:19.070
will really be able to
leverage machine learning,

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:20.990
and, for example,
infrastructures

00:25:20.990 --> 00:25:22.910
such as TensorFlow,
to build very, very

00:25:22.910 --> 00:25:25.160
efficient applications that
connect into a distributed

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:27.116
ledger.

00:25:27.116 --> 00:25:28.240
Then you have the DAML SDK.

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:30.704
And the DAML SDK
was really built

00:25:30.704 --> 00:25:32.870
to allow the developers to
very, very quickly reason

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:35.120
about a distributed application
across many companies,

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:38.510
and not just across
one enterprise.

00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:40.220
And we'll talk a
little bit about that

00:25:40.220 --> 00:25:42.960
in the next few slides.

00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.080
The SDK built those
distributed applications

00:25:45.080 --> 00:25:48.230
that get deployed
onto the DA platform.

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:51.080
And this is a DA platform that's
highly optimized for Google

00:25:51.080 --> 00:25:52.760
Cloud, leveraging
infrastructures

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:58.634
such as the Google Kubernetes
engine and Stackdriver.

00:25:58.634 --> 00:26:00.800
Then you can use managed
services-- either a managed

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:02.510
platform that's managed
by Digital Asset,

00:26:02.510 --> 00:26:04.460
or by one of our
many channel partners

00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:06.930
running on Google Cloud and
managed by a channel partner.

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:08.900
Or you can use your own
Google Cloud account

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:11.660
to deploy either one node
or a full network of nodes.

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:13.826
You can have one node that
connects into an existing

00:26:13.826 --> 00:26:18.327
network and run it on your
own Google Cloud account.

00:26:18.327 --> 00:26:19.910
And last, you can
automate all of this

00:26:19.910 --> 00:26:21.576
through Orbitera,
which really gives you

00:26:21.576 --> 00:26:24.290
a few minutes and
almost a one-click type

00:26:24.290 --> 00:26:28.130
engagement of using the Orbitera
marketplace to quickly launch

00:26:28.130 --> 00:26:30.890
and configure and deploy
a running network.

00:26:33.730 --> 00:26:36.169
So on the development
side, on the IDE,

00:26:36.169 --> 00:26:37.960
the demo-integrated
development environment

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:40.400
provides real-time
feedback to the developers

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:43.880
as they're writing code to
support efficient development

00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:45.617
of correct smart contracts.

00:26:45.617 --> 00:26:47.450
And what we've been
hearing from the channel

00:26:47.450 --> 00:26:50.450
partners in their
usage of the demo

00:26:50.450 --> 00:26:54.620
SDK is that they were
amazed at how fast they

00:26:54.620 --> 00:26:57.940
can go from nothing to
running a POC or an MVP

00:26:57.940 --> 00:27:01.604
on a running distributed
ledger at high scale.

00:27:01.604 --> 00:27:03.770
We're very excited, with
the announcement yesterday,

00:27:03.770 --> 00:27:08.120
that now Google's client
engineers and architects

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:12.576
have the ability-- have
joined our private beta.

00:27:12.576 --> 00:27:14.950
And they have the ability now
to extend to their clients.

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:16.790
So for all of the
Google clients here

00:27:16.790 --> 00:27:19.000
who want to hear more about
blockchain applications,

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.620
you can reach out to your
account representatives

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:22.880
and your client engineers.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:28.100
And they'll be happy to work
and iterate on solutions.

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:29.990
Further than that, not
just MVPs and POCs,

00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:31.490
we've already gone
through a process

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:34.880
of three years of working
with very large, very, very

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:35.877
complex workflows.

00:27:35.877 --> 00:27:37.460
And even there, these
are institutions

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:41.540
that are very, very used to
heavy waterfall development.

00:27:41.540 --> 00:27:43.630
And we've been receiving
amazing feedback

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:45.940
on that front, both
directly and through channel

00:27:45.940 --> 00:27:48.760
partners, on the cycle
times of deploying

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:52.480
new features as they're
creating these workflows.

00:27:52.480 --> 00:27:55.420
So they're used to having
very, very different stages

00:27:55.420 --> 00:27:59.212
of deployment, building
features, testing.

00:27:59.212 --> 00:28:01.420
And now they can really
compress those cycles, again,

00:28:01.420 --> 00:28:03.836
into that same experience that
a developer today in a tech

00:28:03.836 --> 00:28:06.040
company has, having
a running platform

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.230
and very quickly
developing and deploying--

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:10.750
going through that whole process
all the way to developing

00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:14.250
a feature, testing it, having
it signed off by the customer,

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:17.370
and going into QA.

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:20.290
On the testing front, there's
an integrated scenario language,

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:23.290
which gives a full test-driven
development or behavior driven

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:25.900
development and unit
testing ability, which

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:28.324
is baked into the integrated
development environment.

00:28:28.324 --> 00:28:30.740
There's a sandbox that's always
running in the background.

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:32.670
So if you make a mistake,
if you write a bug,

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:35.170
it's always running all of the
unit tests in the background.

00:28:35.170 --> 00:28:38.560
And you'll get an
immediate feedback loop

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:40.720
of that sandbox of a
distributed environment that's

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:43.340
running locally on your laptop.

00:28:43.340 --> 00:28:45.940
So you immediately
get that feedback.

00:28:45.940 --> 00:28:48.370
And you can click
on those scenarios

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:50.730
and essentially actually
get debug tools,

00:28:50.730 --> 00:28:53.090
the transaction graph
explorer in the same view.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:56.500
So the developer can
see the code, the tests,

00:28:56.500 --> 00:28:58.950
any errors in tests, and
the transaction graphics

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:01.030
explorer at the same time,
and have that ability

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:03.400
to go and debug in
one environment.

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:05.650
And you know that if it's
running in that environment,

00:29:05.650 --> 00:29:09.970
you can immediately deploy to a
robust auto scaling and highly

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:12.890
available infrastructure.

00:29:12.890 --> 00:29:15.670
Of course, if you build,
be it an MVP or POC,

00:29:15.670 --> 00:29:18.696
or a large production
full stack solution,

00:29:18.696 --> 00:29:20.320
you need the ability
to demonstrate it,

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.060
or you need the ability
to build UI components.

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.870
So we have in the SDK
auto code generation tools

00:29:25.870 --> 00:29:28.450
that take whatever smart
contracts and business

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:30.040
logic that you
wrote, immediately

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:32.380
creates GRPC bindings so that
you can build applications

00:29:32.380 --> 00:29:35.927
in whatever language you
want to build applications.

00:29:35.927 --> 00:29:37.510
There is a JVM
framework if you wanted

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:39.120
to use R framework
in order to get

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:41.800
all of the high availability
and auto scaling properties out

00:29:41.800 --> 00:29:42.860
of the box.

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:46.570
You get the ability to
dynamically create front ends.

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:48.670
So there is a graph QL back end.

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:51.220
And it auto
generates the schemas

00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:55.270
for that back end and the types.

00:29:55.270 --> 00:29:57.140
And it creates react components.

00:29:57.140 --> 00:29:58.990
So you can immediately
get those components

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:00.410
and build your own UI.

00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:02.890
And, again, the cycle times
on this are really amazing.

00:30:02.890 --> 00:30:04.598
The speed at which
you can get everything

00:30:04.598 --> 00:30:05.800
all the way to a front end.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:09.340
And we have two or three
developers building POCs

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:12.462
that typically took months.

00:30:12.462 --> 00:30:13.920
And, of course,
last but not least,

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:15.211
you want the ability to deploy.

00:30:15.211 --> 00:30:18.930
And here we're not talking about
deploying something locally,

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:20.450
or on one machine
or two machines.

00:30:20.450 --> 00:30:21.290
You really want to deploy.

00:30:21.290 --> 00:30:23.164
And you want to deploy
to a robust Kubernetes

00:30:23.164 --> 00:30:26.810
cluster that's running in,
be it on prem, private cloud,

00:30:26.810 --> 00:30:29.120
or a public cloud.

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:31.250
And in the case of running
it on the Google Cloud,

00:30:31.250 --> 00:30:33.630
running it on a very
highly optimized

00:30:33.630 --> 00:30:36.350
Google Cloud that has
all the tools to operate

00:30:36.350 --> 00:30:37.430
your environment.

00:30:37.430 --> 00:30:39.770
And as of our announcement
yesterday, and very soon

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:42.840
we'll be making this available,
that Obetera deployment,

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:46.910
that makes it really
seamless to go run and start

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:49.472
operating that network.

