00:33:11.820 --> 00:33:15.150
which, fairly early
on, we realized

00:33:15.150 --> 00:33:18.000
could be deployed not just
in the public blockchain

00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:20.940
configuration, which you've
likely heard the most about,

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:22.920
but specifically
in the enterprise.

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:26.060
So we consider ourselves the
creator of the blockchain

00:33:26.060 --> 00:33:29.940
as a service category, which
we kicked off in late 2015

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:34.770
with one of Google's
competitors who I won't name.

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:38.370
And we're very happy to help
bring blockchain as a service

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:42.790
to the Google Cloud
Platform today.

00:33:42.790 --> 00:33:45.750
So we believe also we are the
first to bring a production

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:49.340
blockchain application online
in a Fortune 500 context.

00:33:49.340 --> 00:33:52.680
This is with the world's largest
mining company, BHP Billiton,

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:55.440
in late 2016.

00:33:55.440 --> 00:33:57.570
And through that
effort we learned a lot

00:33:57.570 --> 00:34:00.900
about what it takes
for enterprises

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:02.610
to adopt blockchain technology.

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:06.570
And the fact that enterprise
needs are somewhat orthogonal

00:34:06.570 --> 00:34:11.429
to what you see in the public
networks as far as, one,

00:34:11.429 --> 00:34:14.880
performance, transaction
throughput, latency,

00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:17.280
the complexity of the
business logic present

00:34:17.280 --> 00:34:21.510
in the enterprise, and all the
non-functional requirements--

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:23.610
compliance, privacy, et cetera--

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:28.820
which commercial vendors like
us help our customers through,

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:30.780
where you don't see in
the more open source

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:34.860
focused public blockchain area.

00:34:34.860 --> 00:34:37.889
So in any case, we found that
a number of other vendors

00:34:37.889 --> 00:34:40.300
were trying to solve the
same problems in the Ethereum

00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:41.481
ecosystem.

00:34:41.481 --> 00:34:43.230
One way to think of
what Ethereum has done

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:45.360
is it's not just a
public blockchain,

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:47.670
but it's also a
protocol standard,

00:34:47.670 --> 00:34:49.770
and is something of a platform.

00:34:49.770 --> 00:34:51.870
And we're finding the
Ethereum virtual machine

00:34:51.870 --> 00:34:55.350
and solidity to sort of be like
Java, in the sense that it was

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:58.170
invented for one context,
Java being invented

00:34:58.170 --> 00:34:59.910
for embedded
systems programming.

00:34:59.910 --> 00:35:03.420
But it's being deployed
in many other contexts

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:07.020
and becoming a developer-focused
kind of community standard

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:10.170
that enterprises
are also deploying.

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:16.620
So we got together with several
of our, one, large customers,

00:35:16.620 --> 00:35:19.530
and, two, technology
companies who

00:35:19.530 --> 00:35:21.390
wanted to see the
adoption of sort

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:24.540
of a Java-style
standards-based approach

00:35:24.540 --> 00:35:28.075
to make blockchain technology
a reality in the enterprise.

00:35:28.075 --> 00:35:30.450
So we're on the founding board
of the Enterprise Ethereum

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:32.160
Alliance.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:34.560
And I personally chair the
Technical Steering Committee

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:36.990
that sets out to
define what Ethereum

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:38.820
means in the enterprise.

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:43.970
So a little bit on our platform.

00:35:43.970 --> 00:35:47.290
So we, again, are a
platform provider.

00:35:47.290 --> 00:35:52.810
We sit atop the Google Cloud
and on the Cloud marketplace.

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:56.060
And we see ourselves as
built on open standards.

00:35:56.060 --> 00:35:58.060
So you can think of
blockchains, again,

00:35:58.060 --> 00:36:00.940
as not just platforms,
but protocols, protocols

00:36:00.940 --> 00:36:03.440
for communication
and transactions.

00:36:03.440 --> 00:36:05.470
And so we implement
these protocols

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:08.950
and facilitate the creation
of enterprise applications

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:12.400
across a variety of verticals.

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:13.850
As far as our
platform goes, it's

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:16.940
sort of split into kind
of two core use cases.

00:36:16.940 --> 00:36:18.465
One, the ledger itself.

00:36:18.465 --> 00:36:20.980
So on the lower side
here, a virtual machine

00:36:20.980 --> 00:36:23.050
which does transaction
processing.

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:26.260
Synchronization, which comprises
the peer to peer network.

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:29.170
And the consensus
algorithm allows everyone

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:32.590
to arrive at the
same shared truth.

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:36.490
And the database component,
which allows our customers

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:38.380
to query the blockchain
as they would

00:36:38.380 --> 00:36:40.180
a traditional
relational database.

00:36:40.180 --> 00:36:43.810
And this is all exposed through
a variety of APIs, which

00:36:43.810 --> 00:36:45.940
do things like key management.

00:36:45.940 --> 00:36:48.700
So customers don't have to
think about the nitty gritty

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:52.630
of the lifecycle of
the private keys which

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:56.860
encode the data that's
on the blockchain.

00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:59.360
Querying APIs that are
built out of the box.

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:01.160
So when you write
a smart contract,

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:04.660
you'll have an API for it
that front end developers

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:09.451
can write user interfaces to
without any additional work.

00:37:09.451 --> 00:37:09.950
Et cetera.

